1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,452
A deadline is close, and a senior engineer
proposes to suspend three team rules for

2
00:00:09,492 --> 00:00:14,444
two weeks: small commits, the required
check, and the ban on force pushes to shared

3
00:00:14,484 --> 00:00:17,691
branches. Somebody answers
"those are best practices".

4
00:00:17,731 --> 00:00:22,503
That answer loses the argument, because
it gives no reason, and a rule without a

5
00:00:22,543 --> 00:00:25,329
reason is the first thing
to go under pressure.

6
00:00:25,669 --> 00:00:30,334
The textbook puts it in one line: a practice
you can't justify is a superstition,

7
00:00:30,374 --> 00:00:35,098
and it will be dropped under pressure.
Today each practice gets its mechanism, and

8
00:00:35,138 --> 00:00:38,758
each anti-pattern gets the wrong
mental model that produces it.

9
00:00:38,798 --> 00:00:43,463
Hold on to the question on screen. One
revert, in one line of output, will answer

10
00:00:43,503 --> 00:00:43,637
it.

11
00:00:45,477 --> 00:00:49,150
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:54,140
This video closes the chapter on team
workflows. It has four parts: practices with

13
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:59,192
