16
00:01:08,908 --> 00:01:10,207
Today you finalize it.

17
00:01:10,547 --> 00:01:15,293
Nothing here needs a new mechanism. Every
reason is a fact from an earlier part of

18
00:01:15,333 --> 00:01:19,786
the course: what a commit is, what a ref
is, what a merge brings, what a push

19
00:01:19,826 --> 00:01:24,688
checks. As a reminder: a commit is one saved
snapshot of the project with a message.

20
00:01:25,028 --> 00:01:28,528
A ref is a name that points at
a commit, such as a branch.

21
00:01:28,568 --> 00:01:32,251
A merge brings in every commit
reachable from the other side.

22
00:01:32,291 --> 00:01:37,133
And a push asks the server to move its
ref. The work of this video is to connect

23
00:01:37,173 --> 00:01:39,513
each rule to the fact that pays for it.

24
00:01:41,353 --> 00:01:42,932
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:43,272 --> 00:01:44,684
After this video you can:

26
00:01:45,024 --> 00:01:49,604
Give the mechanism behind each practice:
small commits, meaningful messages,

27
00:01:49,644 --> 00:01:53,617
protected main, required checks,
no force push on shared branches.

28
00:01:53,957 --> 00:01:57,131
Give the root cause behind each
anti-pattern the section lists.

29
00:01:57,471 --> 00:02:00,974
Show with a transcript what a
small commit buys at revert time.

30
00:02:01,314 --> 00:02:05,223
Query commit messages as data:
by trailer, by type, by issue.

31
00:02:05,563 --> 00:02:09,995
Finalize a personal and a team configuration
from the worksheet of Module 5.

32
00:02:11,835 --> 00:02:16,572
Practices, each with its reason. Read this
table as two columns: what breaks without

33
00:02:16,612 --> 00:02:19,245
the practice is the mirror
image of the reason.

34
00:02:19,585 --> 00:02:24,231
Three of these rows answer the hook. Small
commits: because the commit is the unit

35
00:02:24,271 --> 00:02:28,859
of undo, of porting, of bisect and of
review. Revert undoes one commit with a new

36
00:02:28,899 --> 00:02:33,659
commit, cherry-pick copies one commit to
another branch, and bisect searches history

37
00:02:33,699 --> 00:02:38,459
for the commit that broke something. Required
checks: a check that isn't required is

38
00:02:38,499 --> 00:02:38,859
advice.

39
00:02:39,199 --> 00:02:44,845
No force push on shared branches: you
watched in video 168 what the next careless

40
00:02:44,885 --> 00:02:45,617
merge does.

41
00:02:45,957 --> 00:02:50,487
Anti-patterns, each with its root cause.
An anti-pattern is rarely stupidity.

42
00:02:50,527 --> 00:02:55,474
Each one is the reasonable result of a wrong
mental model, and the model is what you

43
00:02:55,514 --> 00:02:55,948
correct.

44
00:02:56,288 --> 00:03:00,655
The teaching point is in the middle column.
When a colleague force-pushes blindly,

45
00:03:00,695 --> 00:03:02,750
the correction is not "do not do that".

46
00:03:03,090 --> 00:03:06,701
It's the missing fact: a rejection is
information about the server's ref.

47
00:03:07,041 --> 00:03:11,426
Code review as a practice. Review is
where a team's knowledge of its code is

48
00:03:11,466 --> 00:03:16,318
exchanged. Finding defects is the smaller
part of its value, and a review that takes

49
00:03:16,358 --> 00:03:18,065
days costs more than it finds.

50
00:03:18,405 --> 00:03:23,916
Four points from section 27.17. Size decides
everything else: every technique that

51
00:03:23,956 --> 00:03:29,264
shortens branches, flags, stacks and small
commits, is also a review technique.

52
00:03:29,304 --> 00:03:34,951
Speed is part of quality: DORA lists
heavyweight and slow review as a pitfall, and a

53
00:03:34,991 --> 00:03:40,638
change that waits two days makes its author
start something else, so agree on a time

54
00:03:40,678 --> 00:03:41,856
to first response.

55
00:03:42,196 --> 00:03:46,613
Say what kind of comment it is:
blocking, a question, or a preference.

56
00:03:46,653 --> 00:03:51,833
Rouan Wilsenach's "Ship / Show / Ask" lets
the author choose per change whether to

57
00:03:51,873 --> 00:03:56,927
merge directly, to open a pull request
and merge without waiting, or to wait for

58
00:03:56,967 --> 00:04:00,747
discussion. It presupposes trust,
good CI and feature flags.

59
00:04:01,087 --> 00:04:05,461
And review the right things. A human
reviewer's attention is best spent on what a

60
00:04:05,501 --> 00:04:09,984
machine can't check: whether the change
does what the issue asked, whether it is in

61
00:04:10,024 --> 00:04:13,417
the right place, what it does on
failure, what it does to data.

62
00:04:13,457 --> 00:04:15,870
Formatting and import
order belong to a tool.

63
00:04:16,210 --> 00:04:21,724
In an ML repository add three questions.
Which data and which evaluation support the

64
00:04:21,764 --> 00:04:27,079
claim in the description? Was a prompt or
configuration change evaluated, or only

65
00:04:27,119 --> 00:04:32,501
edited? And does the pull request change
anything under .github/workflows/, a lock

66
00:04:32,541 --> 00:04:35,939
file or a Dockerfile, which
deserve a second reader?

67
00:04:36,279 --> 00:04:40,964
Quick quiz. A reviewer clicks
"Request changes" on a pull request.

68
00:04:41,004 --> 00:04:45,260
Does that alone block the merge?
A, yes. B, no. Your answer?

69
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:51,316
B, and here is why.

70
00:04:51,656 --> 00:04:55,325
What review doesn't replace:
tests, required checks and rules.

71
00:04:55,365 --> 00:05:00,050
"Request changes" doesn't block a merge,
and CODEOWNERS doesn't enforce review,

72
00:05:00,090 --> 00:05:01,545
unless a ruleset says so.

73
00:05:01,885 --> 00:05:06,993
Commit message conventions. The subject
line says what the commit does, the body

74
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:10,275
says why, and trailers carry
facts that tools read.

75
00:05:10,615 --> 00:05:15,954
Git itself imposes one piece of structure:
the first line, up to the first blank

76
00:05:15,994 --> 00:05:20,996
line, is the subject, which is what git
log --oneline, git shortlog and git

77
00:05:21,036 --> 00:05:24,224
format-patch use. Everything
else is convention.

78
00:05:24,564 --> 00:05:30,166
The most cited one is Chris Beams's seven
rules: separate subject from body with a

79
00:05:30,206 --> 00:05:33,812
blank line. Limit the subject
to about 50 characters.

80
00:05:33,852 --> 00:05:38,422
Capitalize it. Don't end it with a
period. Use the imperative mood.

81
00:05:38,462 --> 00:05:43,789
Wrap the body at 72 characters. Use the
body to explain what and why, not how.

82
00:05:44,129 --> 00:05:50,240
Try it now, for thirty seconds. In any
repository of yours, type git log --oneline

83
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:56,391
--graph -6. It only reads. For each of the
six subjects, ask one question: would a

84
00:05:56,431 --> 00:05:59,916
stranger know what this
commit does? I'll wait.

85
00:06:04,756 --> 00:06:08,144
If one of them says wip or
fix, you're in good company.

86
00:06:08,184 --> 00:06:13,192
Almost every history has some. And you
know the cost now: the message is the only

87
00:06:13,232 --> 00:06:15,311
part of a commit that records why.

88
00:06:15,651 --> 00:06:20,963
Conventional Commits 1.0.0 adds a
machine-readable prefix: type, optional scope, a

89
00:06:21,003 --> 00:06:26,185
colon, a description. fix corresponds to
a PATCH release in Semantic Versioning,

90
00:06:26,225 --> 00:06:31,602
feat to a MINOR one, and a BREAKING CHANGE
footer, or an exclamation mark after the

91
00:06:31,642 --> 00:06:36,041
type, to a MAJOR one. Other types
such as docs or chore are allowed.

92
00:06:36,081 --> 00:06:40,936
The point is that release tooling can
compute the next version number and a

93
00:06:40,976 --> 00:06:42,698
changelog from the history.

94
00:06:43,038 --> 00:06:48,918
Trailers are lines of the form Key: value
at the end of the message: Co-authored-by,

95
00:06:48,958 --> 00:06:54,275
Signed-off-by, which git commit -s adds,
Reviewed-by, or an issue reference.

96
00:06:54,315 --> 00:06:57,587
git interpret-trailers
parses them, and git log

97
00:06:57,627 --> 00:07:01,393
--format='%(trailers:key=Co-authored-by)'
prints them.

98
00:07:01,733 --> 00:07:05,416
What the convention cannot do.
It doesn't make a message true.

99
00:07:05,456 --> 00:07:10,401
fix: typo on a commit that changes a rate
limit passes every commit-message linter.

100
00:07:10,441 --> 00:07:15,265
A convention is enforced the same way as
any other local check: a commit-msg hook

101
00:07:15,305 --> 00:07:20,310
for fast feedback, and a required check or
the squash-merge title for the guarantee.

102
00:07:22,150 --> 00:07:26,959
A picture helps. Think of each practice
as an insurance policy with a named risk.

103
00:07:26,999 --> 00:07:31,629
If you can name the risk and the day it
pays out, the policy survives a budget

104
00:07:31,669 --> 00:07:35,939
discussion. If you cannot, it's
cancelled the first time money is short.

105
00:07:36,279 --> 00:07:40,139
Small commits pay out on the day of
a revert, a backport or a bisect.

106
00:07:40,179 --> 00:07:44,378
A protected main pays out on the day
somebody's push would have rewound it.

107
00:07:44,418 --> 00:07:48,843
The message pays out the day somebody
asks why a line exists and the author has

108
00:07:48,883 --> 00:07:49,125
left.

109
00:07:49,465 --> 00:07:54,032
Where the picture breaks: an insurance
policy costs the same whether or not you

110
00:07:54,072 --> 00:07:58,580
understand it, and a practice does not.
A team that follows a rule without its

111
00:07:58,620 --> 00:08:03,012
reason follows it badly: commits that
are small in lines and still mix three

112
00:08:03,052 --> 00:08:06,394
changes, messages that satisfy
the linter and say nothing.

113
00:08:06,734 --> 00:08:11,760
For anti-patterns, the model is the
root-cause box you have used since video 4,

114
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:15,287
applied to people. Observed
behavior: the anti-pattern.

115
00:08:15,327 --> 00:08:20,032
Root cause: a sentence the person
believes. Correct fix: the missing fact.

116
00:08:21,872 --> 00:08:25,712
The first table turns each reason around:
what breaks without the practice.

117
00:08:26,052 --> 00:08:31,254
The second has the columns "anti-pattern"
and "root cause", from section 27.16.

118
00:08:31,294 --> 00:08:36,363
Write the root cause as the sentence the
person believes, in quotation marks.

119
00:08:38,203 --> 00:08:42,925
Into the lab. The project is promptgate,
and a branch called feature/fallback is

120
00:08:42,965 --> 00:08:44,056
about to be merged.

121
00:08:44,396 --> 00:08:49,595
Step 1: inspect before merging. Three
commands answer "what would this merge bring,

122
00:08:49,635 --> 00:08:53,508
and will it merge cleanly", and
none of them changes anything.

123
00:08:53,548 --> 00:08:57,801
🟢 SAFE. git merge-tree adds unreferenced
objects and touches no ref.

124
00:08:58,141 --> 00:09:03,210
Predict: the branch is called "fallback".
How many commits do you expect, and what

125
00:09:03,250 --> 00:09:06,014
do you expect them to
touch? Say it out loud.

126
00:09:10,854 --> 00:09:14,187
The two-dot git log lists
the commits you would gain.

127
00:09:14,227 --> 00:09:19,407
The three-dot git diff shows the change
since the merge base, which is what a pull

128
00:09:19,447 --> 00:09:24,754
request shows. git merge-tree --write-tree
merges in memory and prints the ID of the

129
00:09:24,794 --> 00:09:29,782
resulting tree. Exit status 0 means no
conflicts. And the first list contains a

130
00:09:29,822 --> 00:09:33,856
surprise: dd57a51, a commit that
raises a limit "for load test".

131
00:09:33,896 --> 00:09:37,930
That's the row "inspect before
you merge" in one line of output.

132
00:09:38,270 --> 00:09:42,866
Step 2: it is merged anyway. 🟡 CAUTION:
a merge moves the current branch.

133
00:09:43,206 --> 00:09:45,585
A merge commit that
preserves the three commits.

134
00:09:45,925 --> 00:09:50,948
Here's that merge as a graph. Three commits
sit on the branch, and the merge brings

135
00:09:50,988 --> 00:09:53,877
all three: the two you
had in mind, and dd57a51.

136
00:09:54,217 --> 00:09:58,425
Step 3: why small commits pay. The
load-test limit reached production.

137
00:09:58,465 --> 00:10:03,340
🟡 CAUTION: git revert adds a commit, which
moves the branch, and removes nothing.

138
00:10:03,680 --> 00:10:07,090
One commit undone, one file
touched, the feature intact.

139
00:10:07,430 --> 00:10:11,813
That's the answer to the question from the
opening: this is what small commits buy.

140
00:10:12,153 --> 00:10:17,181
Step 4: the same branch as one squashed
commit. A squash folds the commits of a

141
00:10:17,221 --> 00:10:21,608
branch into one. Predict: what does
the only available revert remove?

142
00:10:21,648 --> 00:10:22,635
Say it out loud.

143
00:10:27,475 --> 00:10:32,654
Three files, and gateway/fallback.py is
deleted. The only revert available removes

144
00:10:32,694 --> 00:10:35,645
the fallback feature
together with the mistake.

145
00:10:35,985 --> 00:10:40,462
Squash merging is a legitimate choice,
and this is its price: the unit of undo

146
00:10:40,502 --> 00:10:44,748
becomes the pull request. The practice
that follows is not "never squash".

147
00:10:44,788 --> 00:10:48,976
It is "keep pull requests as small as
the commits you would have wanted".

148
00:10:49,316 --> 00:10:51,475
Step 5: messages as data.

149
00:10:51,815 --> 00:10:57,110
--grep="^feat" and the sort | uniq -c
pipeline work only because the prefix is

150
00:10:57,150 --> 00:11:02,650
regular. Add model fallback (#51) is a
GitHub-style squash subject, with the pull

151
00:11:02,690 --> 00:11:08,396
request number as the link back to the
discussion. Note that git shortlog is given a

152
00:11:08,436 --> 00:11:11,952
revision range. Without one
it reads standard input.

153
00:11:12,292 --> 00:11:15,608
Step 6: measuring a
repository. Replay labs/run

154
00:11:15,648 --> 00:11:21,606
ch27/lab-34-1-design-review-evidence. This
is the repository of Lab 34.1, riskscore,

155
00:11:21,646 --> 00:11:27,603
which contains several anti-patterns on
purpose. The lab finds each from the command

156
00:11:27,643 --> 00:11:32,958
line: an anti-pattern you can measure
is one you can put in front of a CTO.

157
00:11:32,998 --> 00:11:38,813
We read what each command measures. What
it means for a design is your work in the

158
00:11:38,853 --> 00:11:41,241
lab. Every command here is 🟢 SAFE.

159
00:11:41,581 --> 00:11:44,846
Branches, by age and
by distance from main:

160
00:11:45,186 --> 00:11:50,788
Each line: the date of the last commit,
how many commits ahead of and behind main,

161
00:11:50,828 --> 00:11:56,086
and the name. A version caveat from the
textbook: the release that introduced

162
00:11:56,126 --> 00:12:01,247
%(ahead-behind:<ref>) in git for-each-ref
wasn't confirmed for the chapter.

163
00:12:01,287 --> 00:12:02,623
It runs on Git 2.55.

164
00:12:02,963 --> 00:12:05,098
Releases, and where the tags sit:

165
00:12:05,438 --> 00:12:10,229
The fix-direction check of video
172, asked of each release branch:

166
00:12:10,569 --> 00:12:14,709
One of the two commands prints a line.
You know what a printed line means.

167
00:12:15,049 --> 00:12:16,512
How integration happens:

168
00:12:16,852 --> 00:12:21,620
Merges against non-merges on main, the
first-parent log with dates, and how far

169
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:23,567
develop is ahead and since when.

170
00:12:23,907 --> 00:12:25,301
What the history contains:

171
00:12:25,641 --> 00:12:30,221
The largest blobs anywhere in history,
and the commits that added certain paths.

172
00:12:30,261 --> 00:12:34,957
Compare the first output with the git
ls-files at the end: something is in history

173
00:12:34,997 --> 00:12:36,689
that is no longer in the tree.

174
00:12:37,029 --> 00:12:38,102
People and messages:

175
00:12:38,442 --> 00:12:42,670
Who commits, which subjects are shorter
than twelve characters, and the signature

176
00:12:42,710 --> 00:12:44,303
status of every commit on main.

177
00:12:44,643 --> 00:12:49,725
Step 7: the squash trap, and the content
check. The lab's failure scenario: you

178
00:12:49,765 --> 00:12:53,290
report feature/calibration
as unmerged, abandoned work.

179
00:12:53,630 --> 00:12:56,993
Three commit-based checks agree
that the branch isn't merged.

180
00:12:57,033 --> 00:13:01,680
The last command shows a commit on main
whose subject ends in a pull request number.

181
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:04,079
Predict what a content
comparison will say.

182
00:13:08,919 --> 00:13:13,631
Merging the branch would produce exactly
the tree main already has: the two IDs are

183
00:13:13,671 --> 00:13:13,975
equal.

184
00:13:14,315 --> 00:13:19,772
Nothing is left to merge. This is the
root cause from video 170, seen from the

185
00:13:19,812 --> 00:13:21,041
